[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In 2013, one of the country\'s most respected conservation magazines ran a feature calling a new wind turbine the future of clean energy. It had no tower, no spinning blades against the sky, just a low funnel-shaped intake pulling wind'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into a generator sitting at ground level, safe to birds, cheap to build, backed by a real patent, and real investment. The Nature Conservancy tested one on a wildlife refuge in the middle of the Pacific. The Michigan'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'National Guard installed several on its own bases. But if you travel to any of those sites today, you will not find a working turbine. You will find torn fabric, a rotor that hasn\'t turned in years, and a company whose phone number'

[00:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has been disconnected since 2017. So, what happened to the wind turbine that was supposed to make towers and blades obsolete? The company was called Sheer Wind, founded in Chaska, Minnesota in 2010 by'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a mechanical engineer named Dr. Darush Ali Alia had spent years around conventional wind turbines and kept coming back to the same list of problems. The generator and gearbox, the'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heaviest and most expensive parts of any turbine, sat hundreds of feet in the air, which meant every repair required a crane and a trained crew willing to work at height. The spinning blades killed'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'birds and bats in numbers that had become a genuine public relations liability for the wind industry. and the whole structure had to be engineered to survive its own weight at the top of a tower nobody could easily reach. Alle\'s'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'answer was to take the generator and the blades out of the sky entirely, funnel the wind into them instead, and patent the result under the name Invellock, short for increased velocity. By his own account, Alle had over two'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decades of research and product development experience behind him before sheer wind. and he brought that same instinct for patent filings and technical credibility to the new venture. The company filed roughly 10'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'patents over its lifetime, building what looked on paper like a defensible intellectual property position for a genuinely novel approach to an old problem. A CFO joined the board. Marketing staff were hired to manage the'

[02:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'growing press attention. This had the shape of a company built to scale, not a garage experiment waiting for its first customer. The technology moved fast from patent to press. A small field unit'

[02:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rated at a few hundred mill went up first to validate the underlying computational models. A larger demonstration unit rated between 1 1/2 and 5 kW followed soon after. Ali\'s'

[02:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'public projections were bigger still. A 90 ft version of the funnel, he said, could match the output of a full 1.8 megawatt utility turbine using a third of the tower height. a seventh of the'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'land and turbine blades eight-fold smaller in diameter, all while producing electricity at least 40% cheaper. Those were the numbers behind the Sierra magazine feature. And they were the numbers that brought the Nature'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Conservancy and the Michigan National Guard on board as flagship clients, lending the project a credibility that a startup rarely earns this early. Other bladeless and low-profile wind concepts were competing for the same spotlight'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around this time. oscillating masts and vortexbased designs among them. What set Shear Wind apart in the early 2010s wasn\'t just the concept. It was that real institutions, a wildlife conservation nonprofit and a branch of'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the US military had agreed to host actual hardware. This wasn\'t a rendering in an investor deck. It was funnels going up on real government land tested by people whose job depended on the numbers being right. So, how is a low'

[03:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'funnel supposed to outproduce a machine 10 times its height? The principle behind involox is genuinely sound physics, at least in isolation. Wind enters through baffles arranged around a'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wide opening at the top of the structure, gathering air from a broad catchment area no single small blade could sweep on its own. As that air is funneled into a narrowing passage, it behaves the way water does when a garden'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hose is pinched. The same volume of fluid is forced through a smaller cross-section and it has nowhere to go but faster. By the time that accelerated air reaches the turbine blades waiting at the base of the funnel, it is moving'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well above the ambient wind speed outside. In theory, extracting more power from a smaller ground level rotor than an equivalent blade would capture in open air. The central insight, the one that made the pitch so compelling,'

[04:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was simple. Stop building machinery to survive at height and instead build a funnel that brings the wind down to machinery you can reach with your own two hands. A 90-ft funnel doing the work of a much taller turbine sounded like an'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engineering shortcut nobody else had thought to take. It is the kind of idea a Nature Conservancy scientist or a National Guard facilities officer could understand from a single diagram which is exactly why it moved from patent'

[05:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'application to real installations as quickly as it did. There was a second selling point built into the design, easy to overlook next to the efficiency claims, but just as central to why institutions signed on. Because the'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rotor sat inside an enclosed duct at ground level rather than spinning exposed on a tower, Shear Wind marketed Invalu as effectively safe for birds and bats, a direct answer to the wildlife mortality concerns that had dogged'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conventional wind farms for years. For a conservation nonprofit specifically, that promise mattered as much as the efficiency numbers. It is one of the reasons the Nature Conservancy was willing to host a test unit on protected'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'land in the first place. But a number in a press release and a number confirmed by an outside party measuring real output are two very different things. And this is where the story of Invalux'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'turns. Sharwin\'s own marketing materials claimed the technology could deliver electricity as low as 1 cent per kilowatt hour at wind speeds as low as 1 mile per hour with a capacity factor'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between 70 and 90%. For comparison, a conventional utility scale turbine spinning in open air with no funnel at all typically runs at a capacity factor closer to 30 to 45%. and needs wind'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speeds of roughly 8 mph just to begin turning. If sheer winds figures were accurate, the company had not just matched conventional wind power. It had leapt past it by a wide margin at a'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fraction of the height and cost. Those were the numbers circulating through investor materials and trade coverage between 2013 and 2016. Presented with the confidence of a company that'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'believed its own engineering, the company layered additional claims on top of the headline efficiency figures. Capital costs were projected at under $750 per installed kilowatt, alongside 50% lower operating and maintenance'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'costs than a conventional turbine, 90% less land use, and blades up to 84% shorter in diameter than what a utility scale turbine of comparable output would need. Shear Wind projected a payback'

[07:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'period of under 5 years requiring no government subsidy to be commercially viable. Taken together, this was not a modest improvement being pitched to investors and conservation partners. It'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was a claim that Invalox had solved nearly every structural weakness of conventional wind power at once, land use, bird safety, maintenance cost, and raw efficiency in a single patented design. So, if the numbers were this'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'good, why did not a single Involox unit ever reach full commercial operation? The answer, unlike most of what this channel covers, is not a story of a good idea buried by an indifferent industry.'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It is a specific documented account of a promise that did not survive contact with independent scrutiny. When wind power researchers and engineers outside the company examined sheer winds public data, the numbers did not hold up. One'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'widely cited independent analysis concluded that based on sheer wind\'s own reported figures, the device was converting moving air into electricity at a rate roughly 18 times worse than a'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conventional turbine of equivalent size, not better. The fluid dynamics explain why. Forcing wind into a narrowing duct requires enough pressure difference to pull air in through the intake rather than have it simply reflect off the'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'opening and flow around the structure instead. A problem that gets worse, not better. At the very low wind speeds sheer wind had advertised as its greatest advantage. The funnel that was supposed to concentrate gentle breezes'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into commercialgrade power was under real conditions fighting against the air it was trying to capture. This is worth sitting with for a moment because it inverts the physics story this channel usually tells. In most Wildline videos,'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'independent testing confirms a forgotten technology works exactly as claimed, and the mystery is why nobody uses it anymore. Here, independent testing is what exposed the gap between the patent'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the performance. The engineering criticism was not vague skepticism from people who disliked the idea. It was a specific quantified estimate built from Sheerwind\'s own published numbers showing the fundamental physical problem'

[09:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'baked into the concept at the exact wind speeds. the marketing leaned on hardest. The company\'s own internal status reports told a similar story before the bankruptcy filing made it official. Of'

[09:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the seven Beta demonstration units Shearwind had installed by its own account, only one at Fort Kuster in Michigan had reached what the company called a precommissioning evaluation stage. The other six had gotten only as'

[09:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'far as having a funnel physically constructed and wind speed tested with no unit ever confirmed generating power at the scale the marketing promised. On December 29th, 2017, Sherwin\'s parent'

[09:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'company filed for Chapter 7 bankruptcy, listing $3.7 million in liabilities against zero remaining assets. The company\'s website had gone quiet since'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'August of that year. Its phone had already been disconnected. There was no ongoing operation left to license the patented technology to anyone. The patent portfolio itself was later sold off separately at a sealed bid'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'liquidation auction to whoever wanted to own the intellectual property of a device that had never worked as advertised. The units themselves were simply left where they stood. One remains near Chaska, Minnesota, the'

[10:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fabric of its intake torn and unrepaired. Several sit abandoned at Michigan National Guard bases and one still stands on Palmyra Atoll, a wildlife refuge a thousand miles south'

[10:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of Hawaii, installed for the Nature Conservancy and never dismantled. A piece of derelict infrastructure sitting on protected land that exists to keep human intrusion out. There is usually a counterargument in this part of a'

[10:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Wildline video, a case that the upfront cost is worth it once you account for the technologies long-term savings. There isn\'t one here. The company never operated long enough or honestly enough to produce the years of real performance'

[11:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data that would let anyone make that case. What is left is a patent nobody is building from a handful of derelict funnels on federal and conservation land and a bankruptcy filing that answers the question more plainly than any'

[11:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'suppression story could. If you are hoping this is the part of the video where the channel hands you a way to build one of these yourself, it isn\'t. And pretending otherwise would be dishonest. There is no working Invalu unit to purchase, no licensed'

[11:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manufacturer carrying the technology forward, and no independent test data confirming the concept performs at any scale beyond a wind tunnel. What this story is actually useful for is a framework, not a blueprint. When a wind'

[11:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technologies most impressive numbers come only from the company selling it, and its highest profile clients are chosen for their credibility rather than their engineering expertise. that is'

[12:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'worth noticing before the money moves, not after. A few concrete questions would have surfaced this earlier than 2017 did. Has anyone outside the company measured the actual output, not the projected output, of an installed unit?'

[12:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Has that data been published anywhere a mechanical or aerospace engineer could review it independently? Are the flagship clients being asked to evaluate the engineering or simply to host the hardware and lend their name to it? None'

[12:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of those questions require an engineering degree to ask. They simply require asking them before a wildlife refuge or a military base agrees to install the equipment. Not years afterward when a bankruptcy filing'

[12:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forces the question into the open. Genuine ducted and shrouded wind research does exist. Studied for decades by aerospace and mechanical engineers and tested in academic wind tunnels with published peer-reviewed results. That'

[12:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'distinction between a physics paper with real measurements attached and a press release with a projection attached is the one lesson this particular story can hand you. Shear wind\'s funnel proved'

[13:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that a compelling idea, a real patent, and two credible institutional clients, can carry a wind turbine further than its own blades ever could. Right up until the moment someone asks to see the'

[13:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electricity bill. A lot of what looks like suppressed technology on this channel really was buried by a system that benefited from the silence. This one wasn\'t. It simply never worked at the scale it promised. And the record'

[13:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shows exactly why. If honest accounting like that is what you come to this channel for, subscribing and sharing helps make sure the difference between a proven technology and an unproven claim keeps getting told this clearly. Next'

[13:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time, we\'re looking at a heating system running quietly under the floors of millions of homes for 3,000 years, still outperforming the furnace in your basement today.'
